<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Estonians</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Estonians are a Finnic people of the eastern Baltic, speaking a language closely related to Finnish, who form the majority of the population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>. Organised in independent parishes and counties until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-crusades">Northern Crusades</a>, they were conquered by German crusaders and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> between 1208 and 1227 and lived for centuries under Baltic German landowners within Danish, Swedish and Russian states. A national awakening in the 19th century led to independence in 1918, lost in 1940 and regained in 1991.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/estonians.jpg" alt="Estonians" width="320" height="166" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Allice Hunter · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Estonian_Diaspora_in_the_World.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
